# PART 5 — MACHINE LEARNING MODELS

Implement and compare the following algorithms.

## MODEL 1 — Convolutional Autoencoder

Purpose:
Reconstruction-based anomaly detection baseline.

Requirements:

- Train on normal images only.
- Reconstruct input images.
- Calculate reconstruction error.
- Generate pixel-level anomaly maps.
- Generate image-level anomaly scores.
- Save checkpoints.
- Support CPU and GPU.
- Support inference on unseen images.

## MODEL 2 — PaDiM

Use a validated Anomalib implementation where compatible.

Requirements:

- Pretrained feature extraction.
- Normal patch feature distribution modeling.
- Mahalanobis-distance anomaly scoring.
- Pixel-level localization.
- Checkpoint or artifact persistence.
- Repeatable inference.

## MODEL 3 — PatchCore

Use a validated Anomalib implementation where compatible.

Requirements:

- Pretrained feature extraction.
- Feature memory bank.
- Coreset sampling.
- Nearest-neighbor anomaly scoring.
- Pixel-level anomaly maps.
- Configurable memory bank settings.
- Saved model artifacts.

Prefer Wide ResNet-50-2 when supported by the selected framework and environment.

## MODEL 4 — FastFlow

Implement as an optional advanced model if compatible with available hardware and time.

Use a validated implementation.

Document the normalizing-flow approach and its inference requirements.

## MODEL MANAGEMENT

Implement a common model interface.

Each model must support:

- Training or fitting.
- Model saving.
- Model loading.
- Prediction.
- Anomaly score generation.
- Anomaly map generation.
- Metadata reporting.

Create a model registry.

Do not silently replace a model with another model if initialization fails.

Report unsupported models clearly.

The initial priority is a working Autoencoder, PaDiM, and PatchCore pipeline.

FastFlow is a secondary priority.

